# Load Generator Deployment (`deploy.yaml`) Documentation

## Overview

This Kubernetes Deployment file deploys the **Load Generator Service** in the OpenTelemetry Demo application.

The Load Generator automatically generates user traffic against the application. It simulates real users browsing products, adding items to carts, and placing orders.

This helps demonstrate:

* Application performance
* Distributed tracing
* Metrics collection
* Logging
* Monitoring dashboards

Without the Load Generator, the application would remain idle and observability tools would show little or no activity.

---

# What is a Load Generator?

A Load Generator is a tool that automatically creates traffic against an application.

Instead of real users:

```text
Real User
     |
     v
Website
```

The Load Generator acts like hundreds or thousands of simulated users:

```text
Load Generator
       |
       v
Frontend Proxy
       |
       v
Frontend
       |
       v
Backend Services
```

In this deployment, the load generator uses **Locust**, a popular load-testing framework.

---

# Architecture Overview

```text
Load Generator (Locust)
          |
          v
Frontend Proxy
          |
          v
Frontend
          |
          +--> Product Catalog Service
          +--> Cart Service
          +--> Checkout Service
          +--> Shipping Service
          +--> Recommendation Service
          |
          v
OpenTelemetry Collector
          |
          v
Grafana / Jaeger
```

---

# 1. API Version and Resource Type

```yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
```

## Purpose

### Deployment

Creates and manages Pods automatically.

Benefits:

* Self-healing
* Rolling updates
* Rollbacks
* Scaling support

---

# 2. Metadata

```yaml
metadata:
  name: opentelemetry-demo-loadgenerator
```

## Purpose

Deployment name:

```text
opentelemetry-demo-loadgenerator
```

Used by Kubernetes to identify and manage this deployment.

---

# 3. Labels

```yaml
labels:
  opentelemetry.io/name: opentelemetry-demo-loadgenerator
  app.kubernetes.io/component: loadgenerator
```

## Purpose

Labels help Kubernetes:

* Identify resources
* Select Pods
* Group components
* Enable monitoring

---

# 4. Deployment Specification

```yaml
spec:
```

Defines how the Load Generator should run.

---

## 4.1 Replicas

```yaml
replicas: 1
```

### Purpose

Runs one Load Generator Pod.

```text
Load Generator Deployment
            |
            v
     Load Generator Pod
```

If the Pod crashes, Kubernetes recreates it automatically.

---

## 4.2 Revision History

```yaml
revisionHistoryLimit: 10
```

### Purpose

Stores the last 10 deployment versions.

Allows rollback during failed updates.

---

# 5. Selector

```yaml
selector:
  matchLabels:
    opentelemetry.io/name: opentelemetry-demo-loadgenerator
```

## Purpose

Identifies which Pods belong to this Deployment.

---

# 6. Pod Template

```yaml
template:
```

Defines how Pods should be created.

---

# 7. Service Account

```yaml
serviceAccountName: opentelemetry-demo
```

## Purpose

The Pod runs using the:

```text
opentelemetry-demo
```

Service Account.

---

# 8. Load Generator Container

```yaml
containers:
  - name: loadgenerator
```

Main container running Locust.

---

## 8.1 Container Image

```yaml
image: ghcr.io/open-telemetry/demo:1.12.0-loadgenerator
```

### Breakdown

```text
Registry : ghcr.io
Project  : open-telemetry/demo
Version  : 1.12.0
Service  : loadgenerator
```

### Purpose

Contains the Locust-based load testing application.

---

## 8.2 Image Pull Policy

```yaml
imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent
```

### Purpose

* Uses local image if available
* Downloads image only when necessary

---

# 9. Container Port

```yaml
ports:
  - containerPort: 8089
```

### Purpose

Exposes the Locust Web UI.

Port:

```text
8089
```

Administrators can view:

* Active users
* Requests per second
* Response times
* Failures

---

# 10. OpenTelemetry Configuration

## Service Name

```yaml
OTEL_SERVICE_NAME
```

Resolved value:

```text
loadgenerator
```

Used to identify telemetry generated by this service.

---

## Collector Name

```yaml
OTEL_COLLECTOR_NAME=opentelemetry-demo-otelcol
```

Specifies the OpenTelemetry Collector.

---

## Metrics Temporality

```yaml
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_METRICS_TEMPORALITY_PREFERENCE=cumulative
```

Exports metrics as cumulative values.

---

# 11. Locust Configuration

## Web Port

```yaml
LOCUST_WEB_PORT=8089
```

### Purpose

Locust Web UI listens on port:

```text
8089
```

---

## Number of Simulated Users

```yaml
LOCUST_USERS=10
```

### Purpose

Creates:

```text
10 Virtual Users
```

These users continuously generate traffic.

---

### Example

```text
User 1 → Browse Products
User 2 → View Cart
User 3 → Checkout
...
User 10 → Search Products
```

---

## Spawn Rate

```yaml
LOCUST_SPAWN_RATE=1
```

### Purpose

Creates users gradually.

```text
1 User Per Second
```

Startup flow:

```text
Second 1 → 1 User
Second 2 → 2 Users
Second 3 → 3 Users
...
Second 10 → 10 Users
```

This prevents sudden spikes.

---

## Target Application

```yaml
LOCUST_HOST=http://opentelemetry-demo-frontendproxy:8080
```

### Purpose

Defines where generated traffic should go.

Target:

```text
Frontend Proxy
```

Flow:

```text
Load Generator
       |
       v
Frontend Proxy
       |
       v
Frontend
```

All simulated user requests enter through the Frontend Proxy.

---

## Headless Mode

```yaml
LOCUST_HEADLESS=false
```

### Purpose

Disables headless mode.

This means the Locust UI remains available.

---

## Auto Start

```yaml
LOCUST_AUTOSTART=true
```

### Purpose

Automatically starts load testing when the container starts.

Without this:

```text
Start Container
      |
      v
Open Locust UI
      |
      v
Click Start
```

With AutoStart:

```text
Start Container
      |
      v
Traffic Begins Automatically
```

---

## Browser Traffic

```yaml
LOCUST_BROWSER_TRAFFIC_ENABLED=true
```

### Purpose

Generates browser-like traffic patterns.

Benefits:

* More realistic traces
* More realistic metrics
* Better frontend telemetry

---

# 12. Python Protocol Buffers

```yaml
PROTOCOL_BUFFERS_PYTHON_IMPLEMENTATION=python
```

## Purpose

Forces Python implementation of Protocol Buffers.

Used for compatibility with telemetry libraries.

---

# 13. Feature Flag Service

## Host

```yaml
FLAGD_HOST=opentelemetry-demo-flagd
```

## Port

```yaml
FLAGD_PORT=8013
```

### Purpose

Allows the Load Generator to interact with feature flags used in the demo.

---

# 14. Telemetry Export Endpoint

```yaml
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://$(OTEL_COLLECTOR_NAME):4317
```

Resolved value:

```text
http://opentelemetry-demo-otelcol:4317
```

### Purpose

Exports:

* Metrics
* Traces
* Logs

to the OpenTelemetry Collector.

---

# 15. Resource Attributes

```yaml
OTEL_RESOURCE_ATTRIBUTES
```

Resolved value:

```text
service.name=loadgenerator,
service.namespace=opentelemetry-demo,
service.version=1.12.0
```

### Purpose

Adds metadata to telemetry.

Used by Grafana and Jaeger to identify the service.

---

# 16. Resource Limits

```yaml
resources:
  limits:
    memory: 1500Mi
```

## Purpose

Maximum memory:

```text
1500 MiB (1.5 GB)
```

### Why Higher Memory?

Load testing generates:

* Thousands of requests
* Large telemetry volumes
* Concurrent user sessions

Therefore the Load Generator needs more memory than most services.

---

# 17. Volume Mounts

```yaml
volumeMounts:
```

No volumes are configured.

### Meaning

The application runs entirely from the container image.

---

# 18. Volumes

```yaml
volumes:
```

No persistent storage is required.

---

# Traffic Generation Flow

```text
Load Generator
       |
       v
Frontend Proxy
       |
       v
Frontend
       |
       +--> Product Catalog
       +--> Cart Service
       +--> Checkout Service
       +--> Shipping Service
       +--> Recommendation Service
```

Every request creates telemetry data.

---

# Observability Flow

```text
Load Generator
       |
       v
Frontend Proxy
       |
       v
Microservices
       |
       v
OpenTelemetry Collector
       |
       +--> Jaeger
       +--> Prometheus
       +--> Grafana
```

---

# Why Load Generator is Important

Without Load Generator:

```text
Application Running
       |
       v
No Traffic
       |
       v
No Traces
No Metrics
```

With Load Generator:

```text
Application Running
       |
       v
Continuous Traffic
       |
       v
Metrics
Logs
Traces
Dashboards
Alerts
```

This makes the demo environment useful for learning observability.

---

# Summary

This Deployment:

* Deploys the Locust-based Load Generator.
* Uses image `ghcr.io/open-telemetry/demo:1.12.0-loadgenerator`.
* Exposes the Locust UI on port `8089`.
* Simulates `10` users.
* Spawns users at `1 user/second`.
* Sends traffic to `opentelemetry-demo-frontendproxy:8080`.
* Automatically starts load testing.
* Generates browser-style traffic.
* Sends telemetry to the OpenTelemetry Collector.
* Limits memory usage to `1500Mi`.

## In Simple Terms

The Load Generator acts like a group of automated users continuously interacting with the OpenTelemetry Demo application. It creates realistic traffic, which generates traces, metrics, and logs that can be viewed in Grafana, Jaeger, and other observability tools. This allows developers to explore and test the full observability stack without requiring real users.
